kwbackup (0.99.62) unstable; urgency=medium

  * feat(hooks): before/after commands per task

    Each task in a scenario (database, storage, archive, incremental) accepts
    before: and after: lists of shell commands, run via /bin/sh -c with
    KWBACKUP_PROJECT, KWBACKUP_ACTION, KWBACKUP_PHASE, KWBACKUP_STATUS and
    KWBACKUP_TASK_FILE in the environment.

    before runs in order and the first failure aborts the task (exit 1, reported
    as a structured notification failure). after runs always - after success,
    after a task error and after a failed before - and its failures are warnings
    that never change the exit code. One hook is limited by runtime.hook_timeout
    (60s); on timeout the whole process group is killed.

    Hooks are skipped for --dry-run and for a disabled task without --force;
    after is skipped when the lock is busy, because nothing was done. Hook output
    goes to the log: stdout at info, stderr at debug, stderr of a failed hook is
    included in the error message.

    --check shows the hook counts per task. Terminology fixed in the docs: the
    YAML file is a scenario, database/storage/archive/incremental are its tasks.

    tests: order and environment, before failure aborts (after still runs),
    after failure is a warning, timeout, dry-run, disabled task (68/68 e2e,
    52 checks).

 -- Karel Wintersky <karel.wintersky@gmail.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 00:33:53 +0300

kwbackup (0.99.61) unstable; urgency=medium

  * feat(notify): mail, webhook and telegram notifications

    One message per run, sent on failure by default: notify.on takes failure,
    success or always, while an empty on, null or no channels switch the whole
    thing off. Channels: SMTP via smtplib, HTTPS POST via urllib, Telegram Bot
    API. No new dependencies and no curl: a token in argv would be visible in ps.

    The text is built from a whitelist: task file path, project (when it differs
    from the file stem), host, kind, status and exit code, duration, per-action
    summaries and structured failures {action, entity, reason} collected by the
    actions - database name, upstream, or a safe reason such as "rclone exited
    with 1" or "No space left on device". Command lines, stderr and keys never
    enter the message, although --debug keeps writing full commands to the log
    on purpose.

    Silence rules: no section, empty on or zero channels send nothing and log
    nothing. --list, --dry-run and exit 4 stay silent; --check only validates.
    Delivery errors are warnings and never change the exit code; notify.retries
    and retry_delay control repeated attempts.

    YAML mapping keys are no longer scalar-converted: `on:` used to arrive as the
    key True (YAML 1.1), which would have silently ignored notify.on.

    tests: webhook on failure, success, empty on, closed port and --check;
    selftest for the message body, gating, failure_reason, mail and telegram
    (49 checks, 62/62 e2e).

 -- Karel Wintersky <karel.wintersky@gmail.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 00:16:17 +0300

kwbackup (0.99.60) unstable; urgency=medium

  * fix(runner): no leftovers when the progress line shrinks

    The redraw wrote "\r" plus the new line; when the numbers or the rate got
    shorter, the tail of the previous, longer line stayed on screen
    ("...tar)r)").

    Every redraw is padded with spaces up to the longest line seen so far and the
    line is wiped at the end. The filename is shortened with an ellipsis to the
    terminal width (measured from stderr, not stdout): a wrapped line breaks the
    \r redraw and leaves a stale row above. The rate is clamped at zero, and 0x0
    terminal sizes fall back to 80 columns.

    tests: progress_line with a width (ellipsis, fits), 41 checks, 57/57 e2e.

 -- Karel Wintersky <karel.wintersky@gmail.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 20:01:29 +0300

kwbackup (0.99.59) unstable; urgency=medium

  * feat(runner): live packing progress on the terminal

    With --verbose the packing and dump phases now draw a line once a second:

      packing 1.2 GiB, 84 MiB/s (site_2026-11-15-18-39-52.tar.zst)

    The size is polled from the output file, so no data is copied through Python
    and rar (which writes the file itself) is covered too. The line is printed
    only when stderr is a tty: cron and systemd logs stay clean, the final size
    still goes to the log.

    In debug mode the line also shows a percentage: the tree is walked once more
    (source_bytes, with include/exclude) and the bytes read by the last stage come
    from /proc/<pid>/io rchar, which for 'tar | zstd' is the uncompressed stream.

    No new dependencies: polling a stat() and /proc replaces pv.

    tests: progress_line format with and without percent, source_bytes with
    exclude and with an explicit include list (36 checks, 57/57 e2e).

