kwbackup (0.99.57) unstable; urgency=medium

  * feat(log): syslog as a sink next to the log file

    log.syslog: true sends records to the system socket (/dev/log, on systemd
    journald; /var/run/syslog and localhost:514/udp as fallbacks). A string instead
    of true is an explicit socket path, which is what containers and tests need.
    log.facility (daemon by default) selects the syslog facility.

    file and syslog are independent sinks: enable both, either one or none. Both
    obey log.level, so runtime.verbose and runtime.debug still affect the console
    only. Records carry the tag kwbackup[pid], which makes journalctl -t kwbackup
    and rsyslog programname rules work.

    A missing socket, a dead one or a failing emit degrades to a single warning
    instead of a logging traceback in the middle of a run.

    --check prints the syslog destination next to the log level and file.

    tests: syslog sink next to the file (facility, tag, level), log.level filter,
    true -> system socket, missing socket, dead socket, bad facility (54/54).

